    "created_at": "2026-09-30T13:32:45Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-09-30T13:32:45Z",
    "body": "Data point at `87c104d` (which includes PR #226): the tuple-estimate stage is still quadratic on inputs with long repeats. For an 8-bit input made of one random half repeated twice (n samples), wall-clock for `ea_non_iid -q f.bin 8` on an Apple M-series machine was 14.4 s at n = 50,000, 85.4 s at 100,000, 362.8 s at 200,000 (×4–6 per doubling), against 1.3 s, 1.4 s, 2.0 s for random input of the same sizes. A 10^6-sample input of this kind did not finish in 20 CPU-minutes. The time is spent in the LRS accumulation loop (`for(t = L[i-1]; t > b; t--)`, `lrs_test.h:273` and `:495`), whose total work grows with n × (longest repeat). Posting here rather than as a new issue since #163 and this one describe the same behaviour; reopen if you consider the residual worth tracking.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
